      "title": "Sentence 4 — a mechanism and two possible consequences",
      "text": "**Original, G:** “This may curb demand, reducing pressure on prices but potentially weakening hiring.”\n\n**Plain-English paraphrase:** More expensive borrowing may reduce spending. That could ease price pressure, but it could also reduce recruitment.\n\n**Structure:** Main clause followed by two coordinated -ing clauses expressing possible consequences.\n\n**Main clause:** “This may curb demand.” “This” refers to the preceding idea that higher borrowing costs discourage new loans.\n\n**Dependent clauses:** “reducing pressure on prices” and “potentially weakening hiring” are non-finite clauses linked by “but.” They describe consequences of the process in the main clause.\n\n**Important grammar:** The participle clauses do not need repeated full subjects. “Potentially” qualifies the hiring effect; “may” already makes the proposed process uncertain.\n\n**Useful language:** curb demand; pressure on prices; weaken hiring.\n\n**Why it sounds academic:** It compresses a causal chain while acknowledging a possible adverse effect.\n\n**New IELTS-style example:** Higher parking charges may discourage driving, reducing congestion but potentially increasing costs for people without public transport.",
