      "title": "Sentence 5 — defining an abstract concept",
      "text": "**Original, H:** “Job security involves confidence that employment will continue, not simply being employed on the survey date.”\n\n**Plain-English paraphrase:** Job security means believing that you will keep your job; having a job on one particular day is not enough.\n\n**Structure:** Subject + verb + an abstract noun with a content clause, followed by a contrast with an -ing clause.\n\n**Main clause:** “Job security involves confidence …” The rest of the sentence specifies the content of that confidence and contrasts it with a weaker condition.\n\n**Dependent clauses:** “that employment will continue” is a content clause complementing “confidence.” “Being employed on the survey date” is a non-finite passive -ing clause in the contrast.\n\n**Important grammar:** This “that” clause is not a relative clause describing a noun; it states what is believed. Compare the actual relative clause in F: “a demographic shift **that develops over decades**,” where “that” stands for “shift.”\n\n**Useful language:** job security; confidence that; not simply.\n\n**Why it sounds academic:** It distinguishes a lasting quality from a temporary observation and gives an operationally useful definition.\n\n**New IELTS-style example:** Educational inclusion involves confidence that students can participate fully, not simply being admitted to a school.",
